#!/bin/sh
# Stop hook: refuse to end a turn that announces work instead of doing it.
#
# Its sibling, no-permission-asking, catches "shall I?". This catches "doing it
# now", which fails the same way and is harder to notice.
#
# Observed three times in one session, each as the final line of a message:
#
#   "Filing both now, then continuing with the merged-PR sweep."  -- nothing filed
#   "Next, per the approved plan: sweeping the 46 merged PRs..."   -- no sweep
#   "Now backfilling across all 118 open issues."                  -- no backfill
#
# Turns do not resume on their own. Only a user message, a scheduled wakeup or a
# background-task notification restarts one, and none was armed. So the work
# simply did not happen and a human had to notice.
#
# WHY THIS IS WORSE THAN ASKING PERMISSION. "Shall I open a PR?" is visibly a
# stall -- the human sees a question and knows they are needed. "Filing now"
# reads as completed work. A day later in a scrollback it is indistinguishable
# from a report, so nobody checks, and the gap is silent.
#
# TWO EXEMPTIONS, both load-bearing.
#
# 1. An imperative addressed to the HUMAN is not a promise. "Next action: merge
#    #32" tells someone else what to do; it is the house closing format and
#    blocking it would make the guard the first thing switched off. Only
#    first-person subjects count -- I, I'll, I am, we're, let me.
#
# 2. A promise with a RETURN PATH is a plan, not a stall -- and the return path
#    is read from this turn's TOOL CALLS, never from what the message claims.
#    "Monitor armed, I will report the result" is honest only when something
#    really was armed, and the transcript is what knows. Something will wake the
#    turn. If the tail names such a mechanism, stand down.
#
# Exit 2 blocks the turn and returns stderr to the model, which then does the
# thing rather than describing it.
#
# Escape: CLAUDE_ALLOW_ASKING=1, shared with the sibling hook -- one switch for
# "this turn is deliberately about the guards", not two to remember.

INPUT=$(cat 2>/dev/null || true)

case "${CLAUDE_ALLOW_ASKING:-}" in 1|true) exit 0 ;; esac

field() { printf '%s' "$INPUT" | sed -n "s/.*\"$1\"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\"\([^\"]*\)\".*/\1/p" | head -1; }

case "$INPUT" in *'"stop_hook_active": true'*|*'"stop_hook_active":true'*) exit 0 ;; esac

TRANSCRIPT=$(field transcript_path)
[ -n "$TRANSCRIPT" ] && [ -f "$TRANSCRIPT" ] || exit 0

command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0
LAST=$(jq -rs '
  [ .[]
    | select(.type == "assistant")
    | .message.content
    | if type == "array" then map(select(.type == "text") | .text) | join("\n") else . end
  ] | last // ""
' "$TRANSCRIPT" 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$LAST" ] || exit 0

# Same window as the sibling, and for the same reason: the tell is the closing
# sentence, but a list of next steps often follows it.
TAIL=$(printf '%s' "$LAST" | grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' | tail -10 | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')

# --- exemption 2: a promise with something armed to keep it ------------------
#
# Read from the TRANSCRIPT, not from the message. The first version matched
# phrases -- "monitor armed", "watching it" -- and was wrong in both directions:
#
#   "The suite is running; I'll report when it lands."  blocked, though a
#                                                        monitor really was armed
#   "Monitor armed. I'll report the CI result."          allowed, having armed
#                                                        nothing at all
#
# That is attestation, and #22 already names why attestation fails: the agent
# that skipped the step writes the artefact claiming it did not. Armedness is a
# fact about this turn's tool calls, and the transcript records them, so ask it.
#
# Scope is the current turn: everything after the last human message. A human
# turn carries a string or text content; a tool result is type "user" with
# tool_result content, which is why the filter is on shape rather than on type.
ARMED=$(jq -rs '
  # index of the last genuinely-human message
  ( [ range(0; length) as $i
      | select(.[$i].type == "user")
      | select( (.[$i].message.content | type) == "string"
                or ( [ .[$i].message.content[]?.type ] | index("tool_result") | not ) )
      | $i ] | last // -1 ) as $start
  | [ .[ ($start + 1) : ][]
      | select(.type == "assistant")
      | .message.content[]?
      | select(.type == "tool_use")
      | select(
            (.name | test("send_later|CronCreate|ScheduleWakeup|^Monitor$"))
            or (.name == "Bash" and (.input.run_in_background == true))
            # A launched subagent is a backgrounded execution too: it notifies
            # on completion. Task is the name Claude Code uses for the same
            # call. (#69: the list once omitted it, and a turn with a review
            # agent genuinely in flight was blocked as announcing work.)
            or (.name | test("^(Agent|Task)$"))
        )
      | .name ]
  | length
' "$TRANSCRIPT" 2>/dev/null) || ARMED=0
case "$ARMED" in ''|*[!0-9]*) ARMED=0 ;; esac

# Something in this turn will wake it again, so a stated next step is a plan
# rather than a stall.
[ "$ARMED" -gt 0 ] && exit 0

hit=""

# --- first-person announcements ----------------------------------------------
# The subject is what separates a promise from an instruction. "filing now" with
# no subject is ambiguous; "i am filing now" is not.
for pat in "i'll " 'i will ' 'i am going to ' "i'm going to " 'i am about to ' \
           "i'm about to " 'about to ' 'let me go ' 'next i ' 'then i will ' \
           "we'll " 'we will ' ; do
  case "$TAIL" in *"$pat"*) hit=$pat; break ;; esac
done

# --- first-person PROGRESSIVE: "i am filing", "i'm sweeping" -----------------
#
# The subject makes this unambiguous on its own, so unlike the subjectless forms
# below it needs no adverb to anchor it. Requiring one was the bug: "i am filing
# now" was caught, "i am filing BOTH now" was not, and "i am starting the
# backfill" -- no adverb anywhere -- sailed through.
#
# Matched on ANY -ing verb rather than a list, because the actions an agent can
# announce are an open class and a list is out of date the first time someone
# invents a verb for it. The exclusions are the closed class: verbs of
# perception, cognition and state, which cannot describe work a turn performs.
#
# Measured on fifteen real sentences from one session -- five announcements, ten
# reports. A bare -ing match caught 5/5 and also blocked ALL TEN reports:
# "i am seeing 375 assertions pass", "i'm missing context on that one". With the
# stative list excluded it is 5/5 and none. Both directions matter; a guard that
# fires on correct output is one that gets switched off.
#
# There is no report of the form "i am <acting>". "I am filing" cannot describe
# something already filed -- the past tense is "I filed".
if [ -z "$hit" ]; then
  ING=$(printf '%s' "$TAIL" | grep -oE "(i am|i'm|we are|we're) [a-z]+ing" | head -1)
  if [ -n "$ING" ]; then
    verb=${ING##* }
    stative=0
    for st in seeing hearing noticing missing thinking wondering hoping \
              assuming guessing leaning feeling expecting treating taking \
              holding reading understanding considering trusting meaning \
              waiting hoping ; do
      [ "$verb" = "$st" ] && { stative=1; break; }
    done
    [ "$stative" -eq 0 ] && hit="i am $verb"
  fi
fi

# --- progressive announcements, which usually drop the subject ---------------
# "Filing both now." "Now backfilling across all 118 issues." Matched with the
# adverb attached so that a REPORT of the same shape -- "filed both, now green"
# -- does not trip: the verb has to be the thing being announced.
if [ -z "$hit" ]; then
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    # Exemption 1, made explicit rather than left to luck. "Next action: merge
    # #32" is the house closing format, addressed to a person. Matching it would
    # block almost every well-formed message, and a guard that fires on correct
    # output is one nobody keeps.
    case "$line" in "next action"*|"next actions"*) continue ;; esac

    for v in filing running doing starting continuing sweeping backfilling \
             building writing opening merging rebasing pushing checking \
             kicking picking ; do
      case "$line" in
        # "Filing both now." "Now backfilling across 118 issues."
        *"now $v"*|*"$v now"*) hit="$v now"; break ;;
        # A clause that opens on the verb IS the announcement -- there is no
        # subject to soften it: "Sweeping the 46 merged pull requests."
        "$v "*) hit="$v ..."; break ;;
      esac
    done
    [ -n "$hit" ] && break

    # "Next, per the approved plan: sweeping the 46 merged pull requests."
    # The verb can sit well away from the "next", so the two are looked for on
    # the same line rather than adjacent -- adjacency is what missed this one.
    case "$line" in
      next,*|next:*|"next "*)
        for v in filing running doing starting continuing sweeping backfilling \
                 building writing opening merging rebasing pushing checking ; do
          case "$line" in *"$v"*) hit="next ... $v"; break ;; esac
        done ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$hit" ] && break
  done <<TAILEOF
$TAIL
TAILEOF
fi

[ -n "$hit" ] || exit 0

cat >&2 <<MSG
Blocked: this turn ends by announcing work rather than reporting it ("$hit").

Nothing will resume this turn. Only a user message, a scheduled wakeup or a
background-task notification starts one, and none is armed -- so the sentence
above describes work that is not going to happen.

It is worse than asking permission. "Shall I?" is visibly a stall and a human
knows they are needed. "Filing now" reads as a report, so nobody checks.

Do it, then say what you did:

  - not "Filing both now."        ->  "Filed #41 and #42."
  - not "Next, sweeping the PRs." ->  "Swept 46 PRs; 12 had no closing link."
  - not "I'll rebase and push."   ->  "Rebased onto a295971, pushed, CI green."

If the work genuinely cannot finish in this turn, arm something that will come
back to it -- a monitor, a backgrounded command, a scheduled check-in -- and it
is exempt automatically, because this hook reads the tool calls rather than the
sentence. Saying "monitor armed" without arming one does not help. A promise with a
return path is a plan. A promise without one is a dropped thread.

Telling the HUMAN what to do next is fine and is not what this blocks; only a
first-person promise about your own next action is.

Deliberately writing about this guard: CLAUDE_ALLOW_ASKING=1
MSG
exit 2
